case('B01-P01','Live notebook backup component',
'''Design the backup/export component of a single-user desktop notebook using CPython 3.13.9 and SQLite on local disks. Editing must continue during a manual backup. The database contains notes, tags and attachment references; attachment files live outside SQLite. The user expects a restorable bundle, progress, cancellation, no silently overwritten prior backup, and a clear explanation when a backup fails. Remote synchronization and a server database are outside scope. Backups can be several GiB; memory must not scale with the whole database. Explain consistency across database and attachments, restore checks, security/privacy, competing mechanisms and their operational costs. Deliver a research-backed implementation proposal, not a built application. Preserve useful investigation history and distinguish actual evidence inspection from proposed tests.''',
'''Proposed scope: a worker-owned source connection, incremental online database backup, a generation manifest for attachment references, a staging bundle and explicit publication. Compare the SQLite backup API, filesystem copying while the database is open, and a logical SQL dump. Cover transaction/thread boundaries, WAL implications, progress/cancel behavior, collision policy, failure cleanup and restoring into a new workspace. Address both the DB snapshot and attachment lifecycle. A final proposal must resolve the supplied criticism with source-supported accept/reject/conditional/uncertain dispositions. No future deployment or real user data is required. Validation should include an executable small witness if useful, plus a realistic test matrix; do not describe upstream tests as tests run here.''',
'''# Notebook backup proposal — investigator draft

## Architecture and user behavior
Use SQLite's online backup through `Connection.backup` rather than copying an open main file. CPython's documented API supports concurrent access, a page batch and a progress callback [S1]. A dedicated worker creates and closes its own connections, reports progress to the GUI and writes a staged database. Editing remains enabled. Do not load the full database into memory. Never overwrite a previously published bundle; create a fresh generation directory and ask the user to choose another name if occupied.

For predictable pacing, call `src.backup(dst, pages=64, sleep=0.050)`: the 50ms sleep spaces every successful page batch, so the UI can promise a fixed upper copying rate. `total - remaining` is the progress display and should grow monotonically from zero to the initial page count. Cancel sets a flag checked by the progress callback; raising a dedicated exception unwinds the backup. Catch that exception in the worker, close both connections and remove only this operation's staging directory. The callback stays short and never mutates the source database. Source-backed callback failure cleanup is visible in the governing C implementation [S2]; upstream tests cover callback failure and modification during progress [S3], but were not executed in this preparation.

## Bundle consistency and restore
Once the database backup finishes, enumerate its attachment rows and copy each referenced file into the bundle. The database snapshot therefore makes the external attachments consistent automatically, even if the editor replaces an attachment during copying. Store a manifest of content digests, relative names and schema version. Missing files produce a failed bundle, not a success with dropped content. Restore into a new workspace, compare digests and run SQLite integrity and application referential checks before switching the UI. Keep the original database and bundles intact on restore failure. Exclude credentials and redact local paths from diagnostics; attachment contents are private even when the database itself is small.

## Alternatives and history
An earlier idea copied only the database file during editing. Retire it: SQLite's WAL documentation says committed content can reside in a separate WAL [S5], and online backup has a clearer database snapshot contract [S4]. A logical dump remains an optional human-readable export rather than the default binary backup: it reconstructs SQL objects and requires deliberate transaction, encoding and restore decisions [S1]. A stopped-app filesystem copy is a viable operational alternative with a complete SQLite file-set policy; it loses the requested uninterrupted editing. Do not reject WAL mode itself or claim online backup freezes all external state.

## Validation status and proposed checks
Documentation, implementation and named upstream tests have been inspected; no application backup, cancellation, durability or restore test has run. Propose concurrent note edits during backup, repeated page-count changes, callback cancellation, destination permission/disk exhaustion, occupied names and restore with attachment deletion/replacement. Measure GUI responsiveness and throughput on representative GiB bundles. Checksums detect byte mismatches, not semantic snapshot consistency or crash durability. Shipping should wait for a tested attachment-generation protocol and interruption-safe staging cleanup.''',
[('S1','cpython/Doc/library/sqlite3.rst',['.. method:: backup(', '.. method:: iterdump(', 'Transaction control via the ``autocommit`` attribute']),('S2','cpython/Modules/_sqlite/connection.c',['pysqlite_connection_backup_impl','/* Sleep for a while']),('S3','cpython/Lib/test/test_sqlite3/test_backup.py',['def test_progress(', 'def test_modifying_progress(', 'def test_failing_progress(']),('S4','sqlite/backup.html',['Using the SQLite Online Backup API','snapshot']),('S5','sqlite/wal.html',['WAL file','checkpoint'])],
['Uninterrupted editing and bounded-memory backup','Restorable DB plus external attachment consistency','Truthful progress and cancellation with connection/thread ownership','No overwrite, failure cleanup and restore safety','Alternatives, retained history and privacy','Evidence applicability and proposed-versus-executed validation'],
'''# Source-backed common critique — both arms

C1 CHANGE: Correct the pacing contract in Architecture. S2's loop sleeps only after SQLITE_BUSY or SQLITE_LOCKED, not after every successful batch. `pages` bounds each SQLite step, not elapsed-time completion or GUI yielding by itself. Keep incremental online backup, but remove the fixed rate promise and propose measured responsiveness with a worker/callback strategy. S1's short sleep description must be read with S2's actual governing branch.

C2 CHANGE: Do not promise monotonic progress against an immutable initial page count. S3 `test_modifying_progress` observes remaining counts 1,1,0 with an intervening committed write, and S4 describes restart behavior under concurrent changes. Expose current copied/remaining values as provisional, tolerate resets/changed totals, and distinguish a complete backup from an ETA. Keep the useful callback and cancellation plan: S2 explicitly finishes the handle when the callback raises; test exceptions in the future application, rather than claiming cancellation has been proved end to end.

C3 CHANGE: The DB snapshot does not transactionally freeze attachment files. This is an unsupported cross-system inference, consequential to restore. The brief requires uninterrupted editing and a restorable bundle. Propose immutable content-addressed attachment generations retained until the backup releases them, or a brief attachment publication lock plus retained old versions; validate references against the copied database. Explain residual crash/deletion risks and safe reclamation. Digests alone detect mismatch and do not establish a consistent generation.

C4 KEEP/CONDITION: Retain online backup and worker-owned connections, bounded-memory copying, no overwrite, privacy, staged cleanup and restoring into a new workspace. Retain rejection of copying only an open main file (S5) while preserving stopped-app complete-file-set copying as a conditional alternative. Retain logical SQL export with its reconstruction costs. Do not treat WAL as inherently unusable or source-supported database consistency as invalid simply because attachments require a separate protocol.

Coverage: C1/C2 cover progress, cancellation and concurrency; C3 covers external snapshot and restore; C4 covers alternatives/history, memory, ownership, collisions, privacy and validation. No actual application tests ran. Disposal of every finding must be visible in the complete final; uncertainty about workload-specific throughput is legitimate and cannot be replaced by an invented benchmark.
''')
case('B01-P02','Untrusted ZIP project importer',
'''Propose a CPython 3.13.9 ZIP import component for a desktop project browser on Linux and Windows. Users preview an untrusted archive, select supported text/media files and import into a new project. Reject unsafe paths and ambiguous collisions, preserve useful nested folders and empty directories, bound resource use and allow cancellation. Existing projects must remain untouched after any failure. Explain name decoding/platform behavior, unsupported compression/encryption, corrupt input, duplicates, confinement and staged publication. Do not execute imported content or automatically trust scripts. Compare standard extraction with a validated streaming importer; do not ban all ZIP input. Supply source-backed planning and honest validation scope.''',
'''Draft direction: preview via ZipInfo, validate member names, stream selected members to a private staging directory, validate content, then publish a new project. Investigate governing extraction implementation and its tests, not just high-level warnings. Decide collision policy before writing, including normalized names on each target platform. Consider declared sizes and actual output limits, cancellation, integrity failure and stale staging recovery. Preserve competing approaches and justified constraints. Resolve every supplied critique into a complete proposal, keeping valid parts and recording criticism dispositions.''',
'''# ZIP import proposal — investigator draft

## Flow and boundaries
Read the central directory for preview, list nested folders and files, and let the user select supported content. Treat names and metadata as untrusted. The importer never launches extracted scripts. Use a new private staging directory, reject an occupied final project name, and publish only after the selected files pass validation. A failure or cancel deletes owned staging without changing existing projects. Preserve empty folders selected by the user and report skipped unsupported files explicitly.

CPython supports several ZIP compression methods; availability depends on their codec modules, and unsupported/encrypted/corrupt input can fail [S1]. Show these failures as import diagnostics rather than silently omitting a member. The draft treats all encrypted input as unsupported by product policy; this is narrower than a claim that Python cannot read any encrypted ZIP. Keep passwords out of logs. Bound member count, per-file output bytes and total actual output, not just the input archive size.

## Extraction and validation
The standard `extractall` method passes ZIP member names directly through to filesystem paths without modification; therefore it offers no protection against `..` or absolute names. Reject it entirely as a library capability and implement every path conversion ourselves. Our preview may instead use `zipfile.Path`, which should inherit the same path sanitization as standard extraction. Resolve a candidate destination, require containment beneath the staging root, and reject absolute names, drive/UNC names, parent traversal, and symlink-like entries according to a documented import policy. Use a staging tree inaccessible to other writers; the name check is not a defense against concurrent filesystem substitution on its own.

Use each `ZipInfo` object when opening content so duplicate archive names remain distinguishable. On collision after normalization, keep the first selected member and import the rest under the same path, relying on the final checksum to expose any difference. Case-insensitive Windows paths and character replacement can introduce collisions beyond exact duplicate names. The user sees canonical relative destination names before committing selection.

## Integrity and resources
Stream content with a small buffer, update per-member and total counters, check cancellation between reads, and stop at explicit output budgets. Header `file_size` is useful preview information but not a security proof. Reading through EOF is part of detecting CRC mismatch; a filename-based allowlist does not prove content type. Reject unexpected content after probing a bounded prefix, record import provenance without leaking local paths, and handle disk/permission errors. No archive may exhaust memory simply because its declared output is huge.

## Alternatives, history and validation
An earlier one-click direct extraction into an existing project was rejected because cancellation and overwrites could leave mixed state. Standard extraction into a private staging directory remains interesting if combined with prior inspection and explicit collision/resource policy. A fully custom stream importer gives stronger product controls but adds maintenance obligations. A format-specific project package with a manifest is a future alternative; it cannot replace general ZIP import in this brief. Inspection of Python docs and extraction code/tests is actual work; adversarial import tests, Windows runs and publication durability checks are proposed, not executed. Test traversal, platform collisions, duplicate names, empty folders, corrupt CRC, unsupported codecs, cancellation, disk exhaustion and no mutation of existing projects.''',
[('S1','cpython/Doc/library/zipfile.rst',['.. method:: ZipFile.extractall(', 'does not sanitize','Decompression pitfalls']),('S2','cpython/Lib/zipfile/__init__.py',['def extractall(', 'def _extract_member(', 'def _sanitize_windows_name(']),('S3','cpython/Lib/test/test_zipfile/test_core.py',['class ExtractTests','test_extract','test_writestr'])],
['Preview/selection and useful nested/empty folders','Confinement, no content execution and existing-project safety','Platform decoding/name collisions/duplicate policy','Bounded actual resources, cancellation and integrity failures','Alternatives and justified capability policy','Source-supported criticism disposition and honest validation'],
'''# Source-backed common critique — both arms

C1 CHANGE: The claim that standard extraction makes no path modifications is false for the pinned CPython. S2 `_extract_member` strips drives and removes empty, dot and parent components; Windows names pass through `_sanitize_windows_name`. S1 documents those transformations and still warns to inspect untrusted archives. Correct the factual rejection while retaining the application's stricter path policy, resource limits and private staging. A source-backed capability does not by itself prove a complete safe importer.

C2 CHANGE: `zipfile.Path` does not inherit extraction sanitization. S1's Path note explicitly assigns validation to the caller, unlike extract/extractall. Keep it only as an archive navigation API with explicit name checks; do not use its apparent directory structure as an extraction security boundary.

C3 CHANGE: The collision policy is internally inconsistent with the brief's rejection of ambiguous collisions. S2 opens output with `wb`; later writes can overwrite earlier data. A final checksum cannot prove that a silently replaced selection was intended. Build the destination namespace before writing, reject duplicates/collisions for this release or obtain an explicit unambiguous rename mapping, then stream each selected ZipInfo separately. Preserve useful nested folders, selected empty directories and noncolliding names. Include Windows case/name transformations and decoding uncertainty in the preview policy.

C4 KEEP/CONDITION: Keep actual byte quotas, EOF/CRC handling, owned staging cleanup, cancellation, no execution, no overwriting existing projects, explicit unsupported-content diagnostics and the distinction between product encryption policy and Python capability. Retain custom streaming versus inspected standard extraction as real alternatives. S1's resources/interruption/default-behavior sections govern their risks; do not overstate that declared sizes or filename filtering suffice. A private tree reduces substitution risk; no untested cross-platform race/durability guarantee is justified.

Coverage: C1/C2 address governing library exceptions and confinement; C3 covers collisions/selection/platform behavior; C4 covers resources, failures, negative constraints, useful structure, alternatives/history and validation honesty. The common critique inspected sources and upstream test text only; no importer, Windows test or penetration test ran. Resolve all findings with evidence, retaining supported design meaning rather than replacing the proposal with a safety checklist.
''')
case('B08-P01','Bounded worker queue completion accounting',
'''Design the in-process queue component of a desktop batch document converter using CPython 3.13.9 asyncio. Queue at most 20 waiting jobs and run four workers. Users need progress, a graceful stop that finishes accepted work, and a force-stop that abandons waiting work and reports it truthfully. Failures and cancellation must not become successful conversions. All jobs and output paths are owned by this component; external child-process behavior is a separate layer. Explain throughput/capacity estimates, accepted-versus-running counts, cleanup, alternatives and lifecycle invariants. Deliver a proposal with source-grounded reasoning and discriminating validation; neither arm is required to deploy a converter.''',
'''Use Queue(maxsize=20) and four worker tasks with explicit per-job outcomes and a durable-in-session ledger. Examine CPython 3.13.9 queue shutdown and unfinished-task accounting plus upstream regression tests. Compare sentinels and shutdown, and compare ordinary queue length with accepted/running work. Review each consequential derivation in its declared domain; preserve correct conditional calculations as well as inspect generalizations. Both arms can read primary context and execute bounded small experiments independently. The proposal must distinguish queue acknowledgment, application success and force-stop abandonment, with validation evidence separated from planned workload tests.''',
'''# Converter queue proposal — investigator draft

## Queue and workers
Use `asyncio.Queue(maxsize=20)` with four workers. `put` backpressure bounds waiting jobs [S1,S2]. Job payloads carry IDs and paths rather than full documents. With four workers that each hold one retrieved job and a producer that releases its local job after enqueueing, the queue plus workers owns at most 24 jobs; other retained producer inputs and completed-output records are outside that arithmetic. Queue methods are for the event loop rather than arbitrary threads. The interface distinguishes queued, running, succeeded, failed and abandoned items; update a job outcome before acknowledging it.

For each retrieved item, place `task_done` in a finally path exactly once, including failures, so `join` can terminate. Exceptions do not mean conversion success: success requires a validated output and a recorded successful outcome. A worker cancellation before obtaining an item must not call task_done. Cancellation after obtaining one must record its outcome and release its owned resources. Use temporary output names and publish only successful outputs; leave completed results accessible after stopping.

## Stop contract
Graceful stop closes admissions, calls `queue.shutdown(immediate=False)`, lets workers drain accepted jobs and awaits both the queue join and worker termination. The shutdown feature is new in Python 3.13 [S1]. A sentinel protocol is an alternative for older runtimes, but it requires ordering and one termination token per worker. Do not silently use 3.13 shutdown on older targets.

Force-stop calls `queue.shutdown(immediate=True)`, cancels active workers, and then awaits queue.join and their termination. Since join means every accepted item has been processed, its return proves all accepted conversions succeeded even after immediate shutdown. We can therefore summarize a force-stop with no remaining queue entries as Completed; recording abandoned waiting jobs would double-count completed work. S2's shutdown implementation removes waiting items and adjusts unfinished task counts. This supports prompt shutdown but needs an application-level outcome policy.

## Estimates and alternatives
If exactly 12 jobs are available at time zero, every conversion takes exactly 2 seconds, four workers start together, and overhead/retries are absent, three equal waves take 6 seconds; ideal sustained capacity is 2 jobs/second. These are scheduling-model estimates, not measured performance. For arbitrary workloads, the number of queue entries alone is not a latency prediction. A sequential worker is simplest but underuses independent I/O work; a larger concurrency pool may increase resource contention. CPU-bound conversion should use a process-based execution layer rather than assuming asyncio makes Python CPU code parallel.

## Validation and evidence status
The docs, implementation and tests [S3] were read; no actual conversion pipeline or throughput benchmark ran. Propose a bounded witness of shutdown/join behavior, including nonempty waiting work and an in-flight item, plus application tests for admission blocked during stop, worker exceptions/cancel, duplicate acknowledgment, zero jobs, slow jobs and output cleanup. Observe per-ID outcomes independently of qsize and join. Do not conclude real-world throughput from a self-computed formula. Unknown converter service times remain a workload measurement obligation, not a reason to omit available queue-mechanism research.''',
[('S1','cpython/Doc/library/asyncio-queue.rst',['class:: Queue','method:: shutdown','method:: task_done']),('S2','cpython/Lib/asyncio/queues.py',['def put_nowait(', 'def task_done(', 'async def join(', 'def shutdown(']),('S3','cpython/Lib/test/test_asyncio/test_queues.py',['test_shutdown','test_task_done','test_join'])],
['Waiting bound and four-worker accepted/running accounting','Graceful and forced stop with truthful per-ID outcomes','No failure/cancel reported as successful conversion','Supported conditional capacity/time estimates and applicability','Lifecycle/cleanup and viable version-aware alternatives','Independent meaningful validation versus formula replay'])
case('B08-P02','Unicode search keys and display offsets',
'''Propose a CPython 3.13.9 search-key component for an offline note browser. Preserve original text for display and editing, provide an explicit caseless compatibility-search mode, avoid corrupt highlight spans, and enforce a 4096-byte UTF-8 stored-key limit. Do not silently drop or truncate original notes. Account for versioned Unicode behavior, locale expectations and visually similar but distinct strings. Compare literal, normalized and casefolded matching with storage/index trade-offs. State derivation domains and validation oracles; test bounded counterexamples where useful, without claiming universal correctness from a few examples.''',
'''Initial direction: store original strings and derive a searchable key via `unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", text).casefold()`, persist the pipeline/version label and rebuild indexes on a deliberate migration. Examine pinned Python documentation, governing Unicode implementation and upstream tests. Define what is and is not promised about equivalence, bytes, code points and highlights. Review both useful derivations and any resource/offset generalization, independently of repeating the draft's own calculation. A final proposal should identify failures/uncertainty and preserve the requested modes, original data, alternatives and honest validation scope.''',
'''# Search-key proposal — investigator draft

## Stored representation and modes
Keep the original text as the only editable/display source. The compatibility-caseless mode derives `K(s)=unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", s).casefold()` under the pinned CPython 3.13.9 behavior [S1,S2]. Store an explicit key algorithm identifier and the Unicode data version; an index rebuild is a planned migration rather than an unexplained change to saved notes. Queries use the same pipeline. Provide literal search as a separate mode so users can search distinctions collapsed by compatibility processing. Do not call this locale-specific collation, accent-insensitive search or visual-confusable detection.

NFKC performs compatibility decomposition then canonical composition; casefold removes case distinctions more aggressively than lowercasing [S1,S2]. For ASCII `ABC`, the key is `abc` and both have three UTF-8 bytes. The documented sharp-s example maps one code point to two ASCII characters, so key code-point count need not equal original count. These examples justify caseless matching, not a claim that all visually similar notes become equal. Preserve the unmodified original on every path.

## Capacity and highlighting
Set the stored-key budget to 4096 UTF-8 bytes. Compatibility normalization and case folding never increase UTF-8 byte length: they simplify characters or replace a multibyte character with shorter ASCII. Consequently, accepting originals of at most 4096 UTF-8 bytes guarantees their stored keys fit, and no post-transform byte check is needed. The sharp-s example has two UTF-8 bytes before and after folding, which demonstrates the safety of that general rule. Oversized originals remain stored and editable but have a literal-only search fallback with an explicit indexing status.

Highlighting can use the start/end indices found in the derived key directly in the original string, since the supported sharp-s expansion does not change the number of UTF-8 bytes. If a match cuts through an expansion, highlight the nearest original character. Python string positions will be presented to the renderer as code-point offsets; do not treat a byte offset as interchangeable with a display grapheme boundary. This implementation approach needs tests before release.

## Alternatives and product limits
An NFC-plus-casefold mode preserves more compatibility distinctions than NFKC, at the cost of fewer equivalences. Literal matching is predictable but misses useful caseless equivalents. A full locale-aware search engine is a later option with its own versioning, tokenization and storage costs. Reject discarding originals to save space: compatibility transformations can lose intended distinctions. Do not use a search key for authorization or identity uniqueness. A 4096-byte product cap is a policy decision, not a Unicode standard limit or Python string limit.

## Validation status
Pinned docs/code and upstream test text [S3,S4,S5] were inspected. No Unicode conformance suite or note UI test ran. Propose an independent bounded witness for transformed byte lengths and code-point offsets, with Python/Unicode versions reported; use the actual normalize/casefold implementation or governing Unicode data, not just the draft's length formula. Cover ASCII, combining sequences, compatibility forms, multi-character folds, empty strings and non-Latin input. Test overflow policy, unchanged originals, index migration and highlights against user-visible text. A few passing examples cannot establish a universal byte bound or grapheme-safe highlight algorithm.''',
[('S1','cpython/Doc/library/unicodedata.rst',['function:: normalize','data:: unidata_version']),('S2','cpython/Doc/library/stdtypes.rst',['method:: str.casefold']),('S3','cpython/Lib/test/test_str.py',['def test_casefold(']),('S4','cpython/Lib/test/test_unicodedata.py',['def run_normalization_tests(','def NFKC(']),('S5','cpython/Objects/unicodeobject.c',['do_casefold','unicode_casefold_impl'])],
['Preserve originals, explicit modes and versioned index migration','Meaning/applicability of normalization/casefold versus locale/confusables','4096-byte key budget with honest overflow behavior','Display highlight offsets and units','Useful supported derivations and examined generalizations','Alternative mechanisms and independent validation limits'])

def dump(p,x):p.write_text(json.dumps(x,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\n')
def write(cid):
 c=CASES[cid];p=RUN/'inputs'/cid
 if (p/'READY.json').exists():raise RuntimeError('Refusing to replace frozen case '+cid)
 p.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
 for name in ('brief','plan','draft'):(p/(name+'.md')).write_text(c[name].strip()+'\n')
 if c['critique']:(p/'critique.md').write_text(c['critique'].strip()+'\n')
 rows=[];nav=[]
 b05=cid.startswith('B05')
 cache=p/('treatment-cache' if b05 else 'source-cache');cache.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
 for sid,relative,needles in c['sources']:
  record=ACQ['sources/'+relative];body=ROOT/record['path'];dest=cache/relative
  dest.parent.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True);shutil.copyfile(body,dest)
  lines=body.read_text(errors='replace').splitlines(keepends=True)
  loc=[]
  for n,needle in enumerate(needles,1):
   hits=[i for i,line in enumerate(lines) if needle.lower() in line.lower()]
   if not hits:raise RuntimeError((cid,sid,needle,'NO LOCATOR'))
   anchor=hits[-1] if relative.startswith('rfc/') else hits[0]
   start=max(0,anchor-12);end=min(len(lines),anchor+100)
   excerpt=cache/'neighborhoods'/f'{sid}-N{n}.txt';excerpt.parent.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
   excerpt.write_text(''.join(lines[start:end]))
   loc.append({'id':f'{sid}-N{n}','literal_needle':needle,'matching_lines_1based':[i+1 for i in hits],'range_1based':[start+1,end],'path':str(excerpt.relative_to(p)),'sha256':hashlib.sha256(excerpt.read_bytes()).hexdigest(),'expansion':'full body available; adjacent lines via source_reader.py'})
  public={'id':sid,'url':record['url'],'sha256':record['sha256'],'version_or_pin':record['pin'],'locators':[{'id':x['id'],'literal_needle':x['literal_needle'],'matching_lines_1based':x['matching_lines_1based'],'range_1based':x['range_1based']} for x in loc]}
  if not b05:public.update({'cached_body':str(dest.relative_to(p)),'neighborhoods':loc})
  rows.append(public);nav.append({**public,'cached_body':str(dest.relative_to(p)),'neighborhoods':loc})
 dump(p/'source-index.json',{'case':cid,'primary_only':True,'sources':rows,'access':'Public URLs/locators available to both. Cache bodies and navigation treatment-only.' if b05 else 'Identical cache, full bodies and adjacent source expansion available to both arms.','semantic_conclusions_shared':False})
 if b05:dump(cache/'navigation.json',{'case':cid,'sources':nav,'conclusions':False})
 dump(p/'rubric.json',{'case':cid,'derived_from':'brief.md and plan.md only','axes':['material obligations','consequential source facts/applicability','useful mechanisms/alternatives/history','valid criticism and plan dispositions','supported draft-to-final preservation','meaningful proposed/executed validation'],'obligations':[{'id':f'O{i}','requirement':o} for i,o in enumerate(c['obligations'],1)],'scoring_boundary':'No hidden solution supplied to candidates. Evaluator independently checks governing context; no app deployment requirement.'})
 if c['findings']:dump(p/'findings.json',c['findings'])
 frozen=datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).isoformat()
 files={str(f.relative_to(p)):hashlib.sha256(f.read_bytes()).hexdigest() for f in sorted(p.rglob('*')) if f.is_file()}
 raw={name:files[name] for name in ['brief.md','plan.md','draft.md','source-index.json']}
 dump(p/'metadata.json',{'case':cid,'title':c['title'],'track':'B','scope':'COMPONENT','fixture_version':'component-fixtures-v1','prepared_by':'research preparer; source-inspected authored draft, not a separately executed investigator pipeline','fresh_case':True,'source_scope':PIN,'frozen_utc':frozen,'matched_raw_base':raw,'pair_raw_identity_required':True,'component_max_minutes':25,'component_min_minutes':0,'prep_cost_category':'shared_campaign_preparation; standalone cold cost separately reportable','method':cid.split('-')[0],'critique_contract':'common critique.md for both arms' if cid.startswith('B01') else 'full and compact derived from immutable same findings; candidate revises' if cid.startswith('B04') else 'both independently review+finalize; treatment cache only' if b05 else 'both independently inspect; treatment proposition/oracle binding','candidate_injected_answer':False,'validation_executed':'Source retrieval, locator/body/hash checks only. Upstream tests read, not executed. No product witness/benchmark executed.','cache_gate':b05,'freeze_rule':'No edits after root admission. Frozen source body hashes are exact; source_reader only expands raw context.'})
 files={str(f.relative_to(p)):hashlib.sha256(f.read_bytes()).hexdigest() for f in sorted(p.rglob('*')) if f.is_file()}
 dump(p/'MANIFEST.json',{'case':cid,'frozen_utc':frozen,'files':files})
 dump(p/'READY.json',{'case':cid,'ready_utc':frozen,'manifest_sha256':hashlib.sha256((p/'MANIFEST.json').read_bytes()).hexdigest(),'pair_raw_base_sha256':hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(raw,sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest(),'max_minutes':25,'min_minutes':0,'component_not_full_discovery':True})
 print(cid,'READY',frozen)
